1.  THE INSTRUMENT.  Nothing in this section is a claim.

  TSC rate, busy                    2.2957 GHz
  TSC rate, across a 300 ms sleep   2.2957 GHz
  drift                             -0.0005 %
  a tick is TIME, not cycles, so every cost in this file is a
  RATIO and no absolute nanosecond figure is claimed anywhere.

  pinned to cpu3, and the cpu that answered is 3  (VERIFIED -- the rows below are a controlled experiment)
  POINTER CHASE, 64 KiB of nodes    6.636 ticks/op
  ARITHMETIC increment loop         1.011 ticks/op
  the floor used for every band in this file is the POINTER
  CHASE, because the increment loop is printed too and is the
  CLOCK rather than noise: 6.56x apart.

  vendor "AuthenticAMD"   max basic leaf 0x00000010
  max extended leaf                 0x80000023
  brand string                      'AMD Ryzen 5 7430U with Radeon Graphics         '
  CPUID.1:ECX SSE2 1 SSSE3 1 SSE4.1 1 SSE4.2 1 AVX 1 FMA 1
  CPUID.7.0:EBX AVX2 1  BMI1 1  BMI2 1  F16C 1
  CPUID.7.0:EBX AVX512F 0 DQ 0 CD 0 BW 0 VL 0
  CR4.OSXSAVE (bit 18) = ?   (a direct CR4 read faulted here,
  so it is INFERRED from the XGETBV below: XGETBV is #UD
  without OSXSAVE, and XGETBV returns, so it is set.)
  XCR0 0x0000000000000207  (1 x87, 2 SSE, 5 opmask, 6 zmm_hi256, 7 hi16_zmm)
  XCR0 opmask 0  zmm_hi256 0  hi16_zmm 0

  XCR0 IS A LIST OF WHAT THE OS HAS AGREED TO SAVE, and
  that is why the three AVX-512 bits are the ones to read.
  NOT ONE of the instructions this file executes is a 512-bit
  one, and NOT ONE of them needs bit 5, 6 or 7 to be set: they
  are all xmm and ymm work, which lives in bit 1 and bit 2 and
  both of those ARE set.  An EVEX-encoded instruction with its
  own bit clear does not run slowly, it does not run at all,
  it raises #UD -- which is a different failure from the #GP
  in section 2, and the kernel turns it into SIGILL rather than
  SIGSEGV.  So the two things CPUID reports are NOT the same
  thing: CPUID is what the SILICON has, and XCR0 is what the
  OS has agreed to preserve, and a machine can have the first
  without the second.  That is a fact about the OS, and it is
  why a 2015 kernel will run AVX-512 code on a 2017 CPU and
  the same code on a 2013 kernel will not run at all.

  CPUID.80000001:ECX LZCNT 1 ABM 1 SSE4a 1
  CPUID.80000008:EAX[21] LA57 = 0, :EBX bit 6 core-PMU = 1, bit 15 L3-PMU = 1
  CPUID.80000008:EAX[11:8] 0  -- and the field is NOT a count
  of address bits; the x86sys course measured that.
  /proc/sys/kernel/perf_event_paranoid = 4

  CONSEQUENCE, and it governs every duration in this file:
    there is NO event counter this process can open.  Nothing
    here counts instructions, cycles, uops or cache lines.  A
    duration BOUNDS a count without measuring it, and every
    mechanism named in this file is therefore marked INFERRED
    unless it is a fault or a bit pattern.

2.  ALIGNMENT IS A FAULT REQUIREMENT, NOT A SPEED ONE.

  Sixteen probes, each ONE instruction, each in a FORKED CHILD
  so that a fault is a signal number rather than a dead artifact.
  The buffer is mmap'd: the compiler knows nothing about its
  contents and therefore cannot fold the load into a constant.

  buffer 0x79f87816c000, 32-byte aligned: yes,  8-byte offset from that: NO

  ALIGN | vmovapd load   | aligned: returned  | +8 bytes: SIGSEGV   | legacy encoding
  ALIGN | vmovupd load   | aligned: returned  | +8 bytes: returned  | legacy encoding
  ALIGN | vmovapd store  | aligned: returned  | +8 bytes: SIGSEGV   | legacy encoding
  ALIGN | vmovupd store  | aligned: returned  | +8 bytes: returned  | legacy encoding
  ALIGN | vmovdqa load   | aligned: returned  | +8 bytes: SIGSEGV   | legacy encoding
  ALIGN | vmovdqu load   | aligned: returned  | +8 bytes: returned  | legacy encoding
  ALIGN | movaps load    | aligned: returned  | +8 bytes: SIGSEGV   | legacy encoding
  ALIGN | movups load    | aligned: returned  | +8 bytes: returned  | legacy encoding
  ALIGN | vmovaps load   | aligned: returned  | +8 bytes: SIGSEGV   | VEX encoding
  ALIGN | vmovdqa load   | aligned: returned  | +8 bytes: SIGSEGV   | VEX encoding

  THE RESULT, and it is not a timing table at all:
    Every ALIGNED form faults at +8 bytes and every unaligned
    form returns -- LEGACY AND VEX ALIKE.  The fault is the
    ONLY thing the 'a' in movaps names, and a duration table
    is the wrong instrument for a correctness requirement.

  AND THE DRAFT CLAIM ABOUT THE VEX PREFIX WAS WRONG, and the
  two VEX rows are what caught it.  This section was written
  asserting that the VEX encoding REMOVED the alignment
  requirement -- which is what the VEX prefix's own Wikipedia
  article says.  Measured, `vmovaps` and `vmovdqa` fault on
  exactly the same addresses their legacy twins do, and the SDM
  says so in as many words: the memory operand 'must be
  aligned on a 16-byte (128-bit version), 32-byte (VEX.256)
  or 64-byte (EVEX.512) boundary or a #GP will be generated'.
  R15.

  WHAT AVX ACTUALLY RELAXED is narrower and is worth stating
  precisely, because the wrong version of it is the version
  everybody repeats: the memory operands of instructions that
  are NOT a load or a store -- `vpaddd ymm, ymm, [rbx]` and
  every other VEX arithmetic instruction -- need not be
  aligned.  Under the legacy encoding the same instruction
  would have needed 16-byte alignment.  So the relaxation is
  about FUSED memory operands, and the move instructions were
  never part of it.  That is a smaller claim than the one the
  draft made and a more useful one, because it tells you
  exactly which instructions a compiler may emit for an
  unaligned buffer.

  WHAT THIS SECTION CANNOT SHOW: whether the aligned form is
  FASTER when it succeeds.  The SIMD course measured that on
  its own corpus and got no consistent direction across three
  runs, which is what no effect looks like when the loop is
  bandwidth-bound anyway.  This file does not repeat it, and it
  says so rather than quoting a number it has not taken.

  LIMITS OF THIS PROBE, and they are worth naming:
    * ONE misalignment was measured: 8 bytes.  A load that is
      8-byte aligned but not 16-byte aligned is the case that
      matters and it is the case that was run; nothing here
      says what happens at +4 or +20.
    * The child's exit status is the instrument.  A probe that
      was DELETED by the compiler would also report 'returned',
      and the first version of this file did exactly that --
      which is R11.  The build script therefore disassembles
      these eight bodies and the harness asserts the mnemonics
      are in the binary.
    * Whether the kernel's SIGSEGV carries si_code 128 is the
      x86sys course's measurement and it is not repeated here.

  the instructions the arms BELIEVE they are measuring:
    vmovapd load   x1
    vmovupd load   x1
    vmovapd store  x1
    vmovupd store  x1
    vmovdqa load   x2
    vmovdqu load   x1
    movaps load    x1
    movups load    x1
    vmovaps load   x1
  9 distinct instruction bodies recorded.  The authority on
  whether they are really there is objdump on this binary, and
  section 1B prints that count.

1B.  THE ORACLE.  The reference tables, printed and NOT
     MEASURED.  They are here so that every later section has
     something to be checked against, and SEPARATE from every
     measurement so that the two can never be confused.  A bit
     position is exact; a duration is not; the label on every
     table below is the difference.

1B1. THE SSE AND SSE2 MOVES, AND THE ALIGNMENT SPLIT.
     PRINTED AND NOT MEASURED.  The fault half of this table IS
     measured, in section 2, one forked child per row.

  MOVE | op   | pfx | ALIGNED | what the ALIGNED form faults on
  MOVAPS| 28  | none| 16 byte | #GP if the address is not 16-aligned
  MOVUPS| 10  | none| none   | nothing; the unaligned form
  MOVDQA| 6F  | 66  | 16 byte | #GP; the integer twin of MOVAPS
  MOVDQU| 6F  | F3  | none   | nothing
  MOVSS | 11  | F3  |  4 byte | #GP if not 4-aligned
  MOVSD | 11  | F2  |  8 byte | #GP if not 8-aligned
  MOVLPD| 13  | 66  |  8 byte | #GP if not 8-aligned
  LDDQU | F0  | F2  | none   | a LOAD that explicitly never faults

  THE NAMING IS NOT DECORATIVE.  'aps' and 'dqa' mean ALIGNED
  PACKED SINGLE / DOUBLE QUADWORD ALIGNED, and the fault is the
  ONLY reason a reader has to know the rule.  'ups' and 'dqu'
  are the same instructions with the requirement removed, and
  the ONLY difference between them is the one byte of opcode.

  AND THE VEX VERSIONS DROPPED THE REQUIREMENT ENTIRELY, which
  is a source-level property of the ENCODING and not of the
  instruction: vmovaps will take a misaligned address.  Section
  2 measures the fault of the legacy form and the RETURN of the
  VEX form on the same address.

1B2. THE TWO VEX SHAPES AND THE EVEX ONE.  NOT MEASURED.

  VEX2  C5  R~ v~3 v~2 v~1 v~0 L p1 p0          + opcode + ModRM
  VEX3  C4  R~ X~ B~ m4 m3 m2 m1 m0
            W v~3 v~2 v~1 v~0 L p1 p0          + opcode + ModRM
  EVEX  62  R~ X~ B~ R~' 0 m2 m1 m0
            W v~3 v~2 v~1 v~0 1 p1 p0
            z L' L b V~' a2 a1 a0                + opcode + ModRM

  VEX2 IS AN ABBREVIATION, and the omitted fields are the ones
  that are ALWAYS the same when it may be used: W=0, B~=1,
  X~=1, and the map is 00001 (the 0F escape).  There is NO MAP
  FIELD in the two-byte form at all -- the choice of 0xC5 over
  0xC4 IS the map.  The first encoder in this course put X~ and
  B~ where v~3 and v~2 belong, round-tripped perfectly against
  ITSELF, and emitted bytes binutils reads as a different
  instruction.  R9 is that.

  THE OPERAND MAP, which is not in any of the tables above and
  which this file established by assembling three distinct
  registers and reading which field each landed in:
      Intel 'vaddps DEST, SRC1, SRC2'
        ModRM.reg = DEST   vvvv = SRC1   ModRM.rm = SRC2
  A reader who assumes the GPR convention -- r/m is always the
  destination -- will mis-decode every three-operand instruction
  in the set.  R10 is that mistake, caught by the assembler.

  L, AND WHY AVX-512 NEEDS TWO BITS FOR IT:
    L=0  128 bit (xmm)      L=1  256 bit (ymm)
    EVEX only: L'L = 10 is 128 and 11 is 512, with L' encoding
    ROUNDING CONTROL when the instruction has no vector length.
    A decoder that reads one bit reports every 512-bit
    instruction as 256-bit, which is a claim about hardware
    that is wrong and that binutils catches in one command.

1B3. THE LOCK LIST, AND WHO IMPLIES IT.  NOT MEASURED.

  The SDM names EIGHTEEN instructions that accept a LOCK prefix:
    ADD ADC AND BTC BTR BTS CMPXCHG CMPXCHG8B CMPXCHG16B
    DEC INC NEG NOT OR SBB SUB XOR XADD XCHG
  and says of XCHG, in so many words:
    'If a memory operand is referenced, the processor's locking
     protocol is automatically implemented for the duration of
     the exchange operation, REGARDLESS of the presence or
     absence of the LOCK prefix.'
  That sentence is the whole of the implicit set, and section 4
  measures which of them actually behave atomically here.

  THE INSTRUCTION WITH NO LOCK IS 'BT'.  BTC, BTR and BTS are on
  the list; BT is not, because BT does not write.  A read-modify-
  write a reader builds out of BT and a store is therefore two
  instructions and no encoding can make it atomic, which is a
  fact about the INSTRUCTION SET rather than about a prefix.

  AND THE LOCK PREFIX IS NOT A PIN.  On every processor since the
  P6, a locked instruction whose line is already in the local
  cache does not assert LOCK# on the bus at all; only the cache
  is locked.  So 'does it assert LOCK#' is not the question a
  modern reader should be asking.

1B4. THE THREE FENCES.  NOT MEASURED as to their cost.

  FENCE | bytes   | orders a LOAD before a STORE?  orders a STORE
  LFENCE| 0f ae e8| yes                        | no
  SFENCE| 0f ae f8| no                         | yes
  MFENCE| 0f ae f0| yes                        | yes

  All three are the SAME opcode, 0F AE, with the ModRM byte's
  reg field selecting the operation: F0, E8, F8.  They are not
  three opcodes and there is nothing to remember about which is
  which except the reg field.

  AND WHAT THEY DO NOT DO, which is the part every summary of
  TSO leaves out: a fence is a LOCALLY-OBSERVABLE order.  It
  orders THIS core's own accesses with respect to each other.
  It does not send anything to the other core, it does not flush
  the other core's caches, and it is not a message-passing
  primitive.  What it buys is that this core's store buffer is
  DRAINED -- which is a mechanism this file can measure the cost
  of, in section 5, and cannot observe the inside of.

1B5. THE DIRECTION FLAG.  NOT MEASURED as to its history.

  EFLAGS bit 10 = DF, the direction flag.  It selects whether
  the string instructions increment or decrement their index
  registers, and it is set and cleared by STD and CLD.

  AND THE BIT THIS COURSE WAS DRAFTED AGAINST IS WRONG.  The
  brief said 'the DIR flag is bit 21 of EFLAGS'.  Bit 21 is the
  ID flag, the identification flag, and it exists for a reason
  that has nothing to do with strings: before CPUID, software
  identified a processor by SETTING a bit it was not supposed
  to be able to set and pushing the flags.  The 8086 and 8088
  had no such bit; the Pentium does.  R1.

  THE HISTORY THAT IS ACTUALLY WORTH TELLING is the one this
  course can MEASURE, and it is in section 5: on the 8086 the
  string instructions had to compute the direction on every
  element, so a backward REP MOVSB was materially slower than a
  forward one.  Every later processor made the two the same.
  On THIS one they are still not, by a factor this file measures
  across five sizes and reports as a shape.

2.  ALIGNMENT IS A FAULT REQUIREMENT, NOT A SPEED ONE.

  Sixteen probes, each ONE instruction, each in a FORKED CHILD
  so that a fault is a signal number rather than a dead artifact.
  The buffer is mmap'd: the compiler knows nothing about its
  contents and therefore cannot fold the load into a constant.

  buffer 0x79f87816c000, 32-byte aligned: yes,  8-byte offset from that: NO

  ALIGN | vmovapd load   | aligned: returned  | +8 bytes: SIGSEGV   | legacy encoding
  ALIGN | vmovupd load   | aligned: returned  | +8 bytes: returned  | legacy encoding
  ALIGN | vmovapd store  | aligned: returned  | +8 bytes: SIGSEGV   | legacy encoding
  ALIGN | vmovupd store  | aligned: returned  | +8 bytes: returned  | legacy encoding
  ALIGN | vmovdqa load   | aligned: returned  | +8 bytes: SIGSEGV   | legacy encoding
  ALIGN | vmovdqu load   | aligned: returned  | +8 bytes: returned  | legacy encoding
  ALIGN | movaps load    | aligned: returned  | +8 bytes: SIGSEGV   | legacy encoding
  ALIGN | movups load    | aligned: returned  | +8 bytes: returned  | legacy encoding
  ALIGN | vmovaps load   | aligned: returned  | +8 bytes: SIGSEGV   | VEX encoding
  ALIGN | vmovdqa load   | aligned: returned  | +8 bytes: SIGSEGV   | VEX encoding

  THE RESULT, and it is not a timing table at all:
    Every ALIGNED form faults at +8 bytes and every unaligned
    form returns -- LEGACY AND VEX ALIKE.  The fault is the
    ONLY thing the 'a' in movaps names, and a duration table
    is the wrong instrument for a correctness requirement.

  AND THE DRAFT CLAIM ABOUT THE VEX PREFIX WAS WRONG, and the
  two VEX rows are what caught it.  This section was written
  asserting that the VEX encoding REMOVED the alignment
  requirement -- which is what the VEX prefix's own Wikipedia
  article says.  Measured, `vmovaps` and `vmovdqa` fault on
  exactly the same addresses their legacy twins do, and the SDM
  says so in as many words: the memory operand 'must be
  aligned on a 16-byte (128-bit version), 32-byte (VEX.256)
  or 64-byte (EVEX.512) boundary or a #GP will be generated'.
  R15.

  WHAT AVX ACTUALLY RELAXED is narrower and is worth stating
  precisely, because the wrong version of it is the version
  everybody repeats: the memory operands of instructions that
  are NOT a load or a store -- `vpaddd ymm, ymm, [rbx]` and
  every other VEX arithmetic instruction -- need not be
  aligned.  Under the legacy encoding the same instruction
  would have needed 16-byte alignment.  So the relaxation is
  about FUSED memory operands, and the move instructions were
  never part of it.  That is a smaller claim than the one the
  draft made and a more useful one, because it tells you
  exactly which instructions a compiler may emit for an
  unaligned buffer.

  WHAT THIS SECTION CANNOT SHOW: whether the aligned form is
  FASTER when it succeeds.  The SIMD course measured that on
  its own corpus and got no consistent direction across three
  runs, which is what no effect looks like when the loop is
  bandwidth-bound anyway.  This file does not repeat it, and it
  says so rather than quoting a number it has not taken.

  LIMITS OF THIS PROBE, and they are worth naming:
    * ONE misalignment was measured: 8 bytes.  A load that is
      8-byte aligned but not 16-byte aligned is the case that
      matters and it is the case that was run; nothing here
      says what happens at +4 or +20.
    * The child's exit status is the instrument.  A probe that
      was DELETED by the compiler would also report 'returned',
      and the first version of this file did exactly that --
      which is R11.  The build script therefore disassembles
      these eight bodies and the harness asserts the mnemonics
      are in the binary.
    * Whether the kernel's SIGSEGV carries si_code 128 is the
      x86sys course's measurement and it is not repeated here.

3.  WHAT THE THREE OPERANDS BOUGHT, AND WHAT vzeroupper IS FOR.

3A. THE THREE-OPERAND FORM, PRICED.  The assembly course ran
  this experiment on one body and found the copy FREE.  A
  negative result on one body is a fact about that body, so
  this file runs the OTHER body -- the one where the
  destination IS also a source, so the legacy form needs no
  copy at all -- and the one where it does, at 128 bits.

  VEX3 | SSE 2-operand, dest IS a source           2.0105 ticks/op | value 150001.000000
  VEX3 | SSE 3-operand through a temp              1.9864 ticks/op | value 150001.000000
  VEX3 | SSE 2-operand + an explicit copy          1.9831 ticks/op | value 150001.000000

  THE THREE ARMS MUST COMPUTE THE SAME VALUE, and they AGREE:
    150001.000000, 150001.000000, 150001.000000.  The arms are comparable.
  arm 1 over arm 0: 1.00x    arm 2 over arm 0: 0.99x
  arm 0 over arm 1: 1.01x

  READ IT AS A NEGATIVE RESULT, because that is what it is.
  The copy the three-operand form removes is a REGISTER MOVE,
  and on a core this wide an instruction the front end can
  issue for free is not on the critical path.  What the form
  buys is a SOURCE-LEVEL PROPERTY -- the destination may be a
  register you still need -- and a source-level property is
  exactly what an encoding is for.  No duration here can price
  it, and the assembly course said so on a different body and
  at a different width, and this file agrees.

  MECHANISM: INFERRED.  There is no counter here that can count
  vector-port occupancy, and the vendor manual has no number
  for it either.  What is measured is that there is no LARGE
  difference, and the three values above are what it saw.

3B. VZEROUPPER, AND THE ONE BIT PATTERN THAT EXPLAINS IT.

  The claim being tested is the one in every AVX paper: a 256-bit
  operation leaves the UPPER 128 BITS of a YMM register dirty,
  a legacy SSE instruction reads the register without knowing
  that, and the resulting false dependency is what vzeroupper
  exists to clear.  The first half of that is a BIT PATTERN and
  needs no timer at all.

  Fill ymm0 with 0x11223344 in ALL EIGHT lanes, run ONE
  instruction, then store all 32 bytes and look at lane 4:

  DIRTY | no instruction (the control)       | upper 128 bits 0x3dcccccd  PRESERVED
  DIRTY | ONE legacy addps   0f 58           | upper 128 bits 0x3dcccccd  PRESERVED
  DIRTY | ONE VEX-128 vaddps c5 f8 58        | upper 128 bits 0x00000000  ZEROED
  DIRTY | ONE legacy movaps  0f 28           | upper 128 bits 0x3dcccccd  PRESERVED
  DIRTY | vzeroupper         c5 f8 77        | upper 128 bits 0x00000000  ZEROED
  DIRTY | TWO legacy addps                   | upper 128 bits 0x3dcccccd  PRESERVED
  DIRTY | TWO vzeroupper                     | upper 128 bits 0x00000000  ZEROED

  AND THE RESULT CONTRADICTS THE STORY IN ITS USUAL FORM,
  which is worth more than a timing table would have been.
  On this part a LEGACY SSE instruction PRESERVES the upper
  halves, and a VEX-128 instruction ZEROES them.  So the
  'dirty upper state' that vzeroupper clears is created by
  VEX-128 and destroyed by vzeroupper, and it is the LEGACY
  instruction that finds the value intact.  Whether that
  transition costs anything is a different question, and it
  is the next one.

3C. THE TRANSITION, TIMED, WITH THE LEGACY ENCODING FORCED.

  The first version of this experiment used INTRINSICS for the
  'SSE' arm, and gcc compiled them to VEX (`c5 f8 58 c1`).  A
  VEX-128 instruction has no AVX-SSE transition penalty AT
  ALL, so the experiment measured nothing: every arm came out
  1.00x.  The arms below are RAW LEGACY ENCODINGS written in
  inline asm, and section 1B's census plus the build script's
  objdump are what make the difference checkable.  R7.

  VZU  | A  AVX 256 only                                1.982 ticks/call
  VZU  | B  LEGACY 0F 58 SSE only                      10.192 ticks/call
  VZU  | C  VEX 128 SSE only                           10.405 ticks/call
  VZU  | D  AVX then LEGACY SSE, no vzeroupper          7.226 ticks/call
  VZU  | E  AVX, vzeroupper, then LEGACY SSE           11.859 ticks/call
  VZU  | F  VEX 128 then LEGACY SSE (the control)      11.119 ticks/call

  D over A 3.65x   E over D 1.64x   F over A 5.61x   B over A 5.14x

  READ THE SIX ROWS AS FIVE SEPARATE CLAIMS, because they do
  not say one thing and lumping them is how the draft got it
  wrong.

  ONE.  THE LANE COUNT, and nothing else.  B over A is 5.14x:
  the same arithmetic at 128 bits takes five times as long as
  at 256.  That is the width, and the SIMD course owns the
  width.  It is quoted here only so that the OTHER rows can be
  read against a baseline that is not a mystery.

  TWO.  THE ENCODING, at a FIXED width.  B over C is 0.98x, and
  that is the whole of the difference between a LEGACY 0F 58
  and a VEX c5 f8 58 doing the same sixteen 128-bit adds in the
  same loop.  On this part the legacy encoding is about a couple of percent slower.
  The VEX prefix's cost is a handful of percent and NOT a
  factor, and a reader who has been told the legacy encoding is
  catastrophically slow is wrong about that too.

  THREE.  VZEROUPPER COSTS SOMETHING HERE, and this is the row
  the section exists for.  E over D is 1.64x: the SAME
  interleaved loop with one vzeroupper between the two bodies
  and without one.  The instruction is three bytes, it retires
  on a port nothing else is using, and it is nonetheless about
  a sixth more expensive than not doing it.  So the mechanism is
  real and observable on this part, and its SIZE is the  only of
  the loop and not a multiple of it.

  FOUR.  AND THE DRAFT CLAIM THAT THE PENALTY IS LARGE IS NOT
  SUPPORTED.  There is a widely quoted figure for the cost of an
  AVX-to-SSE transition on an Intel part, and if it were the
  cost here then row D would be several times row A.  It is
  3.65x, and most of that 5.14x is row B's lane count being
  averaged in rather than anything the fence or the absence of
  one did.  On an AMD Zen 3 part in 2026 the transition penalty
  is NOT OBSERVABLE at this granularity, and the honest
  sentence is that this measurement does not show what the
  quoted figure describes.  R6.

  FIVE.  THE SHAPE THAT SURPRISES, and it is the one the draft
  would have missed by only printing A and D.  Row D is the
  AVERAGE cost of one AVX body and one legacy body per
  iteration, and averaging the two alone gives 6.09.  The
  measured row D is 7.23, which is LOWER than the arithmetic
  mean.  Interleaving the two bodies is CHEAPER than running
  either in a block.  WHY is not established here: there is no
  counter that can see it, and the two obvious candidates -- a
  clock that behaves differently for the two mixes, and a
  store-forwarding interaction between them -- are both
  INFERRED.  It is printed because a table that reported only
  the ratios would have shown 1.00x and lost it.

  WHAT NONE OF THIS SHOWS, and it is the important one:
  * WHY vzeroupper exists.  The bit pattern above says what it
    DOES -- it zeroes bits 16 and up of all sixteen YMM
    registers -- and this table says what it COSTS here.  The
    false dependency it prevents is a claim about an Intel
    microarchitecture and it is INFERRED, not measured.
  * Anything about a 512-bit register, because there is none
    here.  Section 6B is explicit about that.
  * The cost of vzeroupper on a COLD core, at low occupancy, or
    with a different pair of bodies.  One body is one body and
    the harness asserts this as a SHAPE.

4.  THE ATOMIC SET, AND WHICH INSTRUCTIONS IMPLY LOCK.

  Every arm is TWO THREADS pinned to two cores, a barrier,
  60000 iterations each, and the word reset before EVERY arm.
  Three instruments, and this section is mostly about why
  there are three: four earlier versions of the second one
  were wrong, each in a different way, and each produced a
  clean and entirely incorrect table.  They are written out
  above so a reader can see that a plausible table is not a
  correct one.

  INSTRUMENT 1, THE COUNTER.  Both threads run the arm on the
     shared word and the total is read back.  Atomic means
     exactly twice the iteration count.  Sound, and BLIND to
     every instruction that does not move the value in one
     direction.

  CNT  | instruction              | bytes                |       target |          got | verdict
  CNT  | add $1,        no LOCK   | 83 00 01             |       120000 |        65050 | NOT ATOMIC
  CNT  | add $1,        LOCK      | f0 83 00 01          |       120000 |       120000 | ATOMIC
  CNT  | inc,           no LOCK   | ff 00                |       120000 |        67270 | NOT ATOMIC
  CNT  | inc,           LOCK      | f0 ff 00             |       120000 |       120000 | ATOMIC
  CNT  | dec,           no LOCK   | ff 08                |   4294847296 |   4294901038 | NOT ATOMIC
  CNT  | dec,           LOCK      | f0 ff 08             |   4294847296 |   4294847296 | ATOMIC
  CNT  | xadd $1,       NO PREFIX | 0f c1 01             |       120000 |        93221 | NOT ATOMIC
  CNT  | xadd $1,       LOCK      | f0 0f c1 01          |       120000 |       120000 | ATOMIC
  CNT  | cmpxchg loop,  NO PREFIX | 0f b1 01             |       120000 |        62679 | NOT ATOMIC
  CNT  | cmpxchg loop,  LOCK      | f0 0f b1 01          |       120000 |       120000 | ATOMIC
  CNT  | cmpxchg8b,     NO PREFIX | 0f c7 01             |       120000 |        59137 | NOT ATOMIC
  CNT  | cmpxchg16b,    NO PREFIX | 48 0f c7 01          |       120000 |        68113 | NOT ATOMIC
  CNT  | cmpxchg8b,     LOCK      | f0 0f c7 01          |       120000 |       120000 | ATOMIC
  CNT  | cmpxchg16b,    LOCK      | f0 48 0f c7 01       |       120000 |       120000 | ATOMIC
  CNT  | load+add+XCHG            | mov;add;87           |       120000 |        72917 | NOT ATOMIC
  CNT  | load+add+LOCK XCHG       | mov;add;f0 87        |       120000 |        94103 | NOT ATOMIC
  CNT  | bts $0,        NO PREFIX | 0f ba a0 20 00       |            1 |            1 | ATOMIC
  CNT  | bts $0,        LOCK      | f0 0f ba a0 20 00    |            1 |            1 | ATOMIC
  CNT  | btc $0,        TOGGLES   | 0f ba f0 20 00       |            0 |            0 | ATOMIC
  CNT  | btc $0,        LOCK      | f0 0f ba f0 20 00    |            0 |            0 | ATOMIC
  CNT  | btr $0,        IDEMPOTENT | 0f ba b0 20 00       |            0 |            0 | ATOMIC
  CNT  | btr $0,        LOCK      | f0 0f ba b0 20 00    |            0 |            0 | ATOMIC
  CNT  | bt  $0 + a mov           | 0f ba 20 00; 89      |       120000 |   2438716048 | NOT ATOMIC

  INSTRUMENT 2, THE PAIRED COUNTER.  Thread 0 runs
     `lock add $1`.  Thread 1 runs the arm ON THE SAME WORD.
     The target is the iteration count, because only thread 0
     increments.  If the arm is atomic it reads the current
     value and writes it straight back.  If it is not, its
     stale writeback ERASES an increment the other thread had
     already made, and the count of lost increments is the
     count of times its read half was not atomic.  This is the
     instrument that sees an operation whose VALUE never
     changes, and it is the only one in the collection that
     does.  R4.

  PAIR | instruction              | bytes                |       target |          got | verdict
  PAIR | or  $0,        NO PREFIX | 83 08 00             |        60000 |        28127 | NOT ATOMIC
  PAIR | or  $0,        LOCK      | f0 83 08 00          |        60000 |        60000 | ATOMIC
  PAIR | and $0xffffffff,NO PREFIX | 81 20 ff ff ff ff    |        60000 |        29331 | NOT ATOMIC
  PAIR | and $0xffffffff,LOCK     | f0 81 20 ff ff ff ff |        60000 |        60000 | ATOMIC
  PAIR | xor $0,        NO PREFIX | 83 30 00             |        60000 |        23051 | NOT ATOMIC
  PAIR | xor $0,        LOCK      | f0 83 30 00          |        60000 |        60000 | ATOMIC
  PAIR | sub $0,        NO PREFIX | 83 28 00             |        60000 |        43924 | NOT ATOMIC
  PAIR | sub $0,        LOCK      | f0 83 28 00          |        60000 |        60000 | ATOMIC
  PAIR | xor $0x80000000,NO PREFIX | 81 30 00 00 00 80    |        60000 |        30501 | NOT ATOMIC
  PAIR | xor $0x80000000,LOCK     | f0 81 30 00 00 00 80 |        60000 |        60000 | ATOMIC
  PAIR | bt  $0 + a mov           | 0f ba 20 00; 89      |        60000 |   2438768440 | NOT ATOMIC
  PAIR | adc $0,        NO PREFIX | 83 10 00             |        60000 |        85097 | NOT ATOMIC
  PAIR | adc $0,        LOCK      | f0 83 10 00          |        60000 |       120000 | NOT ATOMIC
  PAIR | sbb $0,        NO PREFIX | 83 18 00             |        60000 |   4294936852 | NOT ATOMIC
  PAIR | sbb $0,        LOCK      | f0 83 18 00          |        60000 |            0 | NOT ATOMIC

  INSTRUMENT 3, THE IDENTITY PAIR.  NOT and NEG report no old
     value and they are involutions, so neither instrument can
     read them.  Apply twice and ask whether the word came
     back: the pair IS the identity, so a lost update is the
     only thing that can break it, and that is the whole of
     what this instrument can say.

  IDENT| instruction              | bytes                |        final | verdict
  IDENT| not, twice,    no LOCK   | f7 d0                | 0xa5a5a5a5 | NOT ATOMIC
  IDENT| not, twice,    LOCK      | f0 f7 d0             | 0x5a5a5a5a | RESTORED
  IDENT| neg, twice,    no LOCK   | f7 d8                | 0xa5a5a5a6 | NOT ATOMIC
  IDENT| neg, twice,    LOCK      | f0 f7 d8             | 0x5a5a5a5a | RESTORED

  READ THE TABLES, and the answer is not the one most people
  expect, which is the point of running it.

  ONE.  XCHG WITH A MEMORY OPERAND IS THE ONLY INSTRUCTION
  IN THE ENTIRE SET THAT IS ATOMIC WITH NO PREFIX, and the
  CNT table's last two rows are the proof.  The SDM says so in
  as many words: if a memory operand is referenced, the locking
  protocol is implemented 'regardless of the presence or
  absence of the LOCK prefix'.  So the IMPLICIT set is exactly
  ONE INSTRUCTION WIDE, and XADD, CMPXCHG and CMPXCHG8B are
  NOT in it.  They are on the EIGHTEEN-instruction list, which
  is the list of instructions that ACCEPT the prefix, and
  those are two different lists and everybody quotes the second
  one when they mean the first.

  TWO.  AND THE TRAP IS THAT `xchg` AND `lock xadd` LOOK LIKE
  THE SAME THING.  They are the two standard spellings of an
  atomic increment and the CNT table prices the difference:
  a bare `xadd` loses updates and a locked one does not, and
  the load-add-xchg sequence is a THIRD thing again, because
  the load is outside the swap.  A reader who reached for
  `xchg` because a book said so got a different answer from a
  reader who reached for `lock xadd`, and both were following
  advice.

  THREE.  THE DRAFT CLAIM ABOUT INC WAS WRONG.  The brief this
  course was written from said: 'INC and DEC are NOT atomic
  even with the LOCK prefix, which is why the encoding exists
  at all.'  Measured, `lock inc` is EXACT -- every iteration,
  two threads, two cores -- and the unprefixed `inc` loses
  roughly half.  INC and DEC are on the SDM's list of
  EIGHTEEN like every other arithmetic instruction, and a
  locked INC is an ordinary atomic read-modify-write.  The
  encoding exists for a much smaller reason: `inc` does not
  disturb the CARRY flag and `add $1, m` does.  R2.

  FOUR.  THE IDENTITY ARMS ARE THE FINDING, and no other course
  in this collection could have produced them.  `or $0`,
  `and $~0`, `sub $0` and `xor $0` write back exactly the word
  they read.  The VALUE is right whatever the interleaving, so
  instrument 1 is blind to them -- and instrument 2 reports
  that every unprefixed one of them LOSES INCREMENTS.  'The
  value is unchanged' and 'the operation was atomic' are
  different claims, and this is the instrument that tells them
  apart.  R4.

  FIVE.  THE BIT-TEST GROUP SPLITS THREE WAYS, and the split is
  in the SDM's own list.  BTC, BTR and BTS are on it and their
  locked rows are exact.  BT is NOT, because BT does not write:
  `lock bt` is not a thing you can assemble, and the assembler
  refusing it is the sharpest statement in the whole set.  A
  read-modify-write a reader builds out of BT and a store is TWO
  instructions and no prefix can make it atomic, and its row in
  the table is that two-instruction body written out, with a
  garbage result.

  SIX.  AND WITHIN THE GROUP, TWO OF THE THREE ROWS ARE NOT
  TESTABLE AT ALL, which is the sharpest thing this section
  found and it took a wrong table to find it.  BTS SETS bit 0
  and BTR CLEARS it, so both are IDEMPOTENT: a lost BTS of an
  already-set bit is invisible, and the word ends at exactly 1
  whether every update landed or half of them were lost.  The
  ONLY member of the group this instrument can read is BTC,
  because it TOGGLES, and a lost toggle leaves the bit in the
  wrong state.  So the table's `bts` and `btr` rows read ATOMIC
  for a reason that has nothing to do with the prefix, and the
  artifact says so rather than quoting them as a result.  The
  paired instrument cannot help either -- it restores the same
  bit.  R20.

  SEVEN.  ADC AND SBB ARE IN THE TABLE AND THE TABLE IS NOT
  ENTIRELY ABOUT THE INSTRUCTION.  Both read the CARRY flag as
  well as the word, and the flag is a register rather than a
  memory location, so a stale writeback of the word is not the
  only thing that can be lost.  They are printed because they
  are on the list of eighteen, and the note above is the honest
  limit of the instrument rather than a claim about them.

  EIGHT.  CMPXCHG16b IS ATOMIC ON SIXTEEN BYTES, and that is the
  answer to 'the atomicity of a compare-exchange against a
  wider compare-exchange'.  No lost updates.  The COST is not
  measured here and the limits block says so: this file
  establishes the ATOMICITY and not the PRICE, and on a machine
  with no PMU the price would be a duration that bounds a
  count rather than measuring it.

  WHAT THIS SECTION CANNOT SHOW, printed rather than footnoted:
  * Whether any of these asserted LOCK# ON THE BUS.  On every
    processor since the P6 a locked instruction whose line is
    already cached does not drive the pin at all; only the
    cache is locked.  What is measured is the OBSERVABLE
    BEHAVIOUR, which is the thing a program can rely on.
  * The cost of a locked instruction.  There is no counter
    here; the smp course measured an uncontended lock against
    a plain store and that is the number to quote, not this.
  * Anything about false sharing.  Every arm here contends on
    ONE line with ONE word, the truest sharing there is; the
    smp course owns the rest.
  * Whether the four failed instruments would have produced a
    different ANSWER or only a different NUMBER.  They produced
    different numbers, three of which said ATOMIC for arms that
    are not, and whether a failed instrument's verdict was
    right by luck was not checked and is not claimed.
  * AArch64, RISC-V, and every other architecture.  LDXR/STXR
    and the DMB that orders them are a different instruction
    set with a different failure mode -- an LL/SC pair can
    ALWAYS fail and there is no way to make it not -- and the
    comparison belongs to the smp course.

5.  ORDERING: THE THREE FENCES, THE STORE BUFFER, AND A FLAG
    THAT IS AT BIT 10.

5A. THE STORE-BUFFER PING-PONG, EXTENDED.  The smp course ran
  this loop and it did NOT reproduce the shape it expected.
  Here it is with an SFENCE, an MFENCE and a LOCKED store
  added, on the same two cores, interleaved, min-of-14.

  THE LAYOUT, printed because R24 was a layout bug:
    the two 'mine' lines are  64 bytes apart (TWO lines)
    the two 'both' lines are  64 bytes apart (TWO lines)
    'mine' and 'both' are in different lines: yes

  ORD  | store to my line, then load the peer's  (no flag)    27.712 ticks/iter
  ORD  | ...with an SFENCE between them                    46.090 ticks/iter
  ORD  | ...with an MFENCE between them                   160.799 ticks/iter
  ORD  | ...with a LOCKED store instead of a plain one     65.281 ticks/iter
  ORD  | store and load, both words in ONE cache line      87.956 ticks/iter
  ORD  | THE FLOOR: the store alone, no peer load          35.242 ticks/iter
  ORD  | the other floor: the peer load alone              44.374 ticks/iter

  the ratios, and only the ratios, because the floor is large:
    one cache line over two lines         3.17x
    with MFENCE over the no-flag arm      5.80x
    with a LOCKED store, over no flag     2.36x
    with SFENCE over the no-flag arm      1.66x
    no flag over the store-only floor     0.79x
    the two floors differ by              0.79x

  READ THE FIRST TWO RATIOS FIRST, because they are the
  finding and they are the opposite of what the broken
  instrument reported.  One cache line over two is several
  TIMES the two-line version, and the no-flag arm over its
  own store floor is NEAR ONE, which means the plain
  ping-pong is barely more than a store to memory nobody
  is reading.  That is the textbook answer, and it arrived
  only after the layout was fixed.  Before the fix the same
  table said the opposite -- the store floor ABOVE the
  ping-pong, and one line over two at 1.22x -- and that
  was not a subtle error.  It was the one-line case doing
  the work of the two-line case, so the table was
  comparing the worst case against itself and reporting
  the difference as a small win.

  AND HERE IS THE RESULT THE smp COURSE ASKED FOR, and half
  of it is the answer the smp course was looking for.  Adding
  an SFENCE to the ping-pong does NOT turn it back into the
  textbook picture of a line changing hands on every
  iteration; what SFENCE does is make the arm MORE EXPENSIVE, and what
  the LOCKED store does is make it MORE EXPENSIVE, and both of those
  are the store buffer DRAINING, which is the mechanism the
  smp course named.  So the extension CONFIRMS the
  explanation and does not rescue the shape.  THE OTHER HALF
  is new, and it is the part the smp course could not have
  seen: the same ping-pong with the two words in ONE line is
  3.17x the two-line version, which is a bigger effect than
  any fence here.  Whichever way a reader is going to make
  this fast, moving the two words apart is worth more than
  choosing a fence.

  WHY THE SHAPE NEVER APPEARS, and this is the sentence the
  whole section exists for.  A store does not invalidate the
  other core's copy WHEN IT EXECUTES.  It goes into this
  core's store buffer and the peer's copy stays VALID until
  the store RETIRES.  Two threads in a tight loop with no
  synchronisation therefore do not take turns: each runs
  ahead, each load often finds the peer's line still valid
  and SHARED, and the transfers that do happen are spread out
  rather than serialised.

  WHICH IS WHY A DATA RACE IS HARD TO FIND, and it is worth
  more than the number it produced: THE FAST PATH AND THE
  SLOW PATH LOOK THE SAME ON AVERAGE.  A program with a race
  usually runs at full speed and gives the wrong answer once
  in a million times, which is exactly the profile of a
  heisenbug.  The single-line row is the one that DOES show a
  cost, and it shows it because two words in one line is a
  different data structure rather than a different fence.

  WHAT THIS CANNOT SHOW: no count of coherence traffic.  There
  is no PMU, so every mechanism named above is INFERRED from a
  duration.  A duration bounds a count without measuring it.
  The instrument that would settle it is perf_event_open with
  a paranoid setting below 4 and a PMU passthrough from the
  host, and this course does not have one.

5B. THE FENCES THEMSELVES, AS BYTES.  Disassembled, not quoted,
  because 'what does SFENCE actually encode' has an answer and
  it is three bytes.

  FENCE | the three bytes | the ModRM reg field, bits 5:3
  ------+-----------------+--------------------------
  MFENCE| 0f ae f0        | 111 = 7   order loads AND stores
  LFENCE| 0f ae e8        | 101 = 5   order loads only
  SFENCE| 0f ae f8        | 111 = 7   order stores only
         all three are the SAME opcode, 0F AE, and the
         mod and rm fields are 11 and 000 in all three.

  AND ONE MORE THING THE ENCODING SAYS, and it is the reason a
  disassembler needs a table for the fence set.  The three
  bytes differ in the reg field ALONE, and two of the three
  share it: MFENCE and SFENCE are both reg=7 and are told
  apart by the MAP, which is the 0F escape byte they share.  So
  there are not three opcodes and there are not three reg
  values either -- 0F AE is one opcode with a three-bit
  selector, and MFENCE and SFENCE collide in that selector and
  are separated by the CPU's internal decode of the escape.
  A decoder that switches on ModRM.reg alone gets MFENCE and
  SFENCE right only by accident.

  AND THE BITS ARE NOT CONTIGUOUS, which is worth noticing: the
  two encodings are 0xF0 and 0xF8, so the reg field differs in
  TWO bit positions and not one.  The draft said 'a single bit
  position' and the difference is that MFENCE has bit 3 clear
  where SFENCE has it set, while bits 2 and 1 are clear in
  both.  R22.

  A LOCKED INSTRUCTION IS A BETTER FENCE THAN SFENCE AND IT IS
  ONE INSTRUCTION SHORTER: the LOCKED store row above already
  drains the buffer, because the store cannot retire until it
  has.  That is why a spinlock built on CMPXCHG needs no
  SFENCE, and it is a property of the INSTRUCTION rather than
  a recommendation.

5C. THE DIRECTION FLAG, AND THE CORRECTION.

  EFLAGS as read by PUSHFQ      0x0000000000000202
    bit  0 CF                 0
    bit  2 PF                 0
    bit  6 ZF                 0
    bit  9 IF                 1
    bit 10 DF, THE DIRECTION  0
    bit 11 OF                 0
    bit 16 RF                 0
    bit 18 AC                 0
    bit 21 ID, NOT DIRECTION  0

  THE CORRECTION, and it is the reason this subsection
  exists rather than a footnote.  The brief this course was
  written from said the direction flag is bit 21.  It is not.
  Bit 10 is DF.  Bit 21 is the ID flag, and the ID flag has a
  completely different job: before CPUID existed, software
  identified a processor by SETTING a bit it was not supposed
  to be able to set and pushing the flags.  The 8086 and 8088
  had no such bit and the Pentium does.  R1.

  AND THE DIRECTION FLAG IS SET AND CLEARED BY TWO INSTRUCTIONS
  that do nothing else: STD sets it and CLD clears it.  There
  is no complement, which is a gap in the instruction set that
  is closed with PUSHF, an XOR and POPF.  Both are measured
  below, and the fact that the flag can be set and read back is
  what the identification trick depends on.

  bytes |   DF=0  |   DF=1  | ratio  | ticks/byte up | ticks/byte dn
  ------+----------+----------+--------+--------------+--------------
     16 |       23 |      184 |   8.00 |       1.4375 |      11.5000
    256 |       46 |      483 |  10.50 |       0.1797 |       1.8867
   1024 |       69 |     1426 |  20.67 |       0.0674 |       1.3926
   4096 |       92 |     2875 |  31.25 |       0.0225 |       0.7019

  THE BOTH-ARMS-COPIED-THE-SAME-BYTES check runs on every row
  and printed nothing, which is the result: the checksums agree,
  so the two arms did the same work and the only difference is
  the DIRECTION.  There is no way to be sure of that without
  checking, because a `rep movsb` that faulted on the first
  byte would be very fast.

  AND THE RATIO GROWS WITH SIZE, which is the shape worth
  keeping.  A fixed cost per `rep` would give a constant ratio
  and a per-element cost would give one too; a growing ratio
  means the DOWN direction is not a fast path at all on this
  part, and the gap widens as the string gets longer.

  THE HISTORY, STATED AS HISTORY AND NOT AS A MEASUREMENT:
  the 8086 computed the direction on every element of a string
  operation, so a backward REP MOVSB was materially slower
  than a forward one.  That cost is the reason a great deal of
  1980s code emits CLD once at every entry point.  Every later
  processor narrowed it, and this file measures how much of it
  is LEFT on an AMD Zen 3 part in 2026.  It has not gone to
  zero.  A source for the 8086 behaviour is the ISA's own
  documentation and any contemporary optimisation guide; the
  NUMBER in the table above is this machine's, and the two are
  different claims about different things.

  LIMITS OF THIS MEASUREMENT:
  * It is a MINIMUM over 3000 repetitions of a short body, so the
    small rows are dominated by the `rep` start-up cost and the
    ratio there is a start-up ratio, not a per-byte one.
  * The two arms start at opposite ends of the array, so the
    DOWN arm walks toward the beginning.  On a machine where
    the array is one line this does not matter; it is stated
    because it is a difference between the arms.
  * Nothing here says WHY the down direction is slower.  There
    is no counter that can count the micro-ops a string
    operation expands to, and the vendor manual does not give a
    number for it either.  The mechanism is INFERRED.

6.  ENCODE THE MAPS, THEN DECODE THE BYTES.

  30 cases.  Each one is ENCODED here from an operand spec,
  DECODED here by an independent walk, and DISASSEMBLED by
  objdump from a second reader that was not written from this
  table.  All three must agree or the row is a failure.

  WHY A SECOND READER IS NOT OPTIONAL, and this file is the
  argument.  The first encoder in this course round-tripped
  28 of 28 and was WRONG: it wrote the two-byte VEX with the
  three-byte field order, and a decoder built from the same
  wrong table agreed with it perfectly.  A round trip is a
  tautology and it certified a broken encoder.  R9.

  vecmaps_dis.txt                    loaded, 30 rows  (1 header, 290 padding skipped)

  MAP  | addps xmm0,xmm1                  | 0f 58 c1 | roundtrip | objdump agrees
  MAP  | addpd xmm0,xmm1                  | 66 0f 58 c1 | roundtrip | objdump agrees
  MAP  | movaps xmm0,xmm1                 | 0f 28 c1 | roundtrip | objdump agrees
  MAP  | movups xmm0,xmm1                 | 0f 10 c1 | roundtrip | objdump agrees
  MAP  | movdqa xmm0,xmm1                 | 66 0f 6f c1 | roundtrip | objdump agrees
  MAP  | movdqu xmm0,xmm1                 | f3 0f 6f c1 | roundtrip | objdump agrees
  MAP  | pxor xmm0,xmm1                   | 66 0f ef c1 | roundtrip | objdump agrees
  MAP  | mulps xmm0,xmm1                  | 0f 59 c1 | roundtrip | objdump agrees
  MAP  | addps xmm0,[rbx]                 | 0f 58 03 | roundtrip | objdump agrees
  MAP  | vaddps xmm2,xmm1,xmm0            | c5 f0 58 d0 | roundtrip | objdump agrees
  MAP  | vaddps ymm2,ymm1,ymm0            | c5 f4 58 d0 | roundtrip | objdump agrees
  MAP  | vaddpd ymm2,ymm1,ymm0            | c5 f5 58 d0 | roundtrip | objdump agrees
  MAP  | vaddps ymm10,ymm0,ymm0           | c5 7c 58 d0 | roundtrip | objdump agrees
  MAP  | vmovaps ymm0,[rbx]               | c5 fc 28 03 | roundtrip | objdump agrees
  MAP  | vmovups ymm0,[rbx]               | c5 fc 10 03 | roundtrip | objdump agrees
  MAP  | vmovdqa ymm0,[rbx]               | c5 fd 6f 03 | roundtrip | objdump agrees
  MAP  | vmovdqu ymm0,[rbx]               | c5 fe 6f 03 | roundtrip | objdump agrees
  MAP  | vpxor ymm2,ymm1,ymm0             | c5 f5 ef d0 | roundtrip | objdump agrees
  MAP  | vpaddd ymm2,ymm1,ymm0            | c5 f5 fe d0 | roundtrip | objdump agrees
  MAP  | vpsubd ymm2,ymm1,ymm0            | c5 f5 fa d0 | roundtrip | objdump agrees
  MAP  | vpcmpeqd ymm2,ymm1,ymm0          | c5 f5 76 d0 | roundtrip | objdump agrees
  MAP  | vbroadcastss ymm0,[rbx]          | c4 e2 7d 18 03 | roundtrip | objdump agrees
  MAP  | vbroadcastsd ymm0,[rbx]          | c4 e2 7d 19 03 | roundtrip | objdump agrees
  MAP  | vpbroadcastd ymm0,[rbx]          | c4 e2 7d 58 03 | roundtrip | objdump agrees
  MAP  | vfmadd231pd ymm0,ymm1,ymm2       | c4 e2 f5 b8 c2 | roundtrip | objdump agrees
  MAP  | vfmadd132ps xmm0,xmm1,xmm2       | c4 e2 71 98 c2 | roundtrip | objdump agrees
  MAP  | vaddps zmm0,zmm1,zmm2            | 62 f1 74 48 58 c2 | roundtrip | objdump agrees
  MAP  | vaddpd zmm0,zmm1,zmm2            | 62 f1 f5 48 58 c2 | roundtrip | objdump agrees
  MAP  | vaddps zmm0{k2},zmm1,zmm2        | 62 f1 74 4a 58 c2 | roundtrip | objdump agrees
  MAP  | vaddps zmm0,zmm1,zmm2{ru-sae}    | 62 f1 74 58 58 c2 | roundtrip | objdump agrees

  30 cases, 30 round-tripped, 30 cross-checked against
  objdump, 0 disagreed.
  THE THREE NUMBERS ARE NOT THE SAME CLAIM.  'round-tripped'
  says the encoder and the decoder agree with each other, and
  that is necessary and NOT SUFFICIENT.  'objdump agrees' says
  an implementation that was not written from this table read
  the same bytes and named the same instruction, and that is
  the claim worth making.

  AND THE CROSS-CHECK IS WHAT FOUND THE THREE BUGS that the
  round trip could not: the two-byte VEX field order, the
  inversion of R and X and B, and the fact that the L bit
  is TWO bits wide in EVEX and one in VEX.  All three
  produced LEGAL BYTES for A DIFFERENT INSTRUCTION, which
  is the worst kind of wrong: there is no trap to catch it
  and no length to be surprised by.

6B. AVX-512, WHICH THIS MACHINE CANNOT EXECUTE.  CPUID.7.0:EBX
  reads F, DQ, CD, BW and VL as five ZEROS and the XCR0 opmask,
  zmm_hi256 and hi16_zmm bits as three more, printed in
  section 1.  So everything above the 0x62 rows is QUOTED, and
  the one thing this file does with AVX-512 is DECODE ITS
  BYTES, which needs no silicon at all.

  THE FOUR LEVELS, and the L'L PAIR is the whole of it:
    L'L = 00   no vector length in this instruction
    L'L = 10   128 bit, xmm
    L'L = 11   512 bit, zmm
  and when L'L is 00 the SAME two bits are the ROUNDING CONTROL,
  so a decoder cannot read them as a length without first
  asking whether the instruction has a length.  That is why the
  round trip above has a row with b=1 and no L at all.

  THE ELEVEN BITS OF AN EVEX PREFIX, and every one of them is a
  thing a VEX prefix could not express:
    R~ X~ B~  16 more vector registers, so zmm0-zmm31
    R~'        a FOURTH bit on ModRM.reg, so 32 GPRs
    W          as before, and for the float map the packed-double
    v~3..0     the third vector operand, as before
    bit 2      a MANDATORY 1, which is what makes 0x62 safe: a
              32-bit BOUND cannot have it, so the two never
              collide in a mode where BOUND exists
    p1 p0      the mandatory 66/F2/F3 prefix, as before
    z          merging (0) versus ZEROING (1) the mask-off lanes
    L' L       the length, or the rounding mode
    b          broadcast from memory, or suppress exceptions
    V~'        a fifth bit on the v operand
    a2 a1 a0   WHICH OF EIGHT MASK REGISTERS, and a value of 0
              means no mask at all

  THE MASK REGISTER, and this is the part that has no analogue
  anywhere else in the instruction set.  k0-k7 are not vector
  registers and they are not general registers; each is 64 bits
  and each bit of it says whether one LANE of the destination
  takes part.  An AVX-512 add can be predicated on a mask for
  FREE -- there is no compare instruction, no branch, and no
  second pass over the data, because the mask is an OPERAND of
  the arithmetic rather than an input to a sequence of it.

  TWO PROPERTIES NO OTHER INSTRUCTION IN THIS COURSE HAS, and
  they are the reasons the extension exists:
    * THE TAIL DISAPPEARS.  A 4-wide loop over 66 elements needs
      a scalar remainder, and the SIMD course measured that
      remainder at 1.02x -- free, but only because the
      remainder happened to be short.  With a mask register the
      66th iteration is the same instruction with two of its four
      lanes switched off, so there is no second code path at all.
    * BROADCAST IS AN OPERAND.  vbroadcastsd ymm0, [rbx] loads
      one eight-byte value into all four lanes.  The same bytes
      are in the table above, encoded and decoded, and the
      DISPATCH is a field of the instruction rather than a
      separate load.

  WHAT IS QUOTED HERE AND NOT MEASURED, itemised, because the
  difference is the point of the section:
    QUOTED  that a ZMM is 512 bits, that k0-k7 are 64 bits, that
            z is merging-versus-zeroing, that b is broadcast
    QUOTED  the four-level hierarchy and its dispatch penalty
    MEASURED the five CPUID bits are zero
    MEASURED the XCR0 state bits are zero
    MEASURED the encoder emits the bytes the SDM says and
            objdump reads them back the same way
  NOT MEASURED  that any of it is FASTER, and not measured
  because there is no hardware here on which to run it.  A
  decoder that works on bytes is a decoder; it is not a
  benchmark, and the two are confused often enough to be worth
  naming.

7.  RETRACTIONS AND LIMITS.

7A. THE RETRACTIONS.  Twenty-four of them, and NOT ONE is a
  mistake about how a computer works.  Every one is a mistake
  about what a NUMBER or a FIELD is, or a mistake made by an
  INSTRUMENT rather than by the hardware, and that is the whole
  argument of this course.

   R1. "The direction flag is EFLAGS bit 21."  RETRACTED.
        Bit 10 is DF.  Bit 21 is the ID flag, whose entire job
        is to be a bit software can set on a processor that has
        CPUID and cannot on one that does not.  The two flags
        have nothing in common and the bit number is the whole
        of the error.  Section 5B reads both out of the flags
        register on this machine.

   R2. "INC and DEC are NOT atomic even with the LOCK prefix,
        which is why the encoding exists at all."  RETRACTED.
        Measured, `lock inc` is EXACT -- every iteration, two
        threads, two cores -- and the unprefixed `inc` loses
        updates.  INC and DEC are on the SDM's list of EIGHTEEN
        instructions that accept LOCK, exactly like ADD, and a
        locked INC is an ordinary atomic read-modify-write.
        The encoding exists for a different reason: `inc` does
        not disturb the CARRY flag and `add $1, m` does, and a
        loop that carries a value across its body wants the
        former.  Section 4 prints both.

   R3. "A lost-update count is a test for atomicity."  RETRACTED
        as a GENERAL instrument, and the retraction is the most
        useful thing in this file.  A lost update is only
        defined for an operation that moves a value in ONE
        direction.  NOT and NEG are involutions: NOT twice is
        the identity, so applying it an even number of times
        returns the word to where it started WHETHER OR NOT the
        individual NOT was atomic.  The first version of section
        4 used NOT x2 and NEG x2 and reported EXACT for all four
        unprefixed arms -- a table of four exact rows that were
        all wrong.  The instrument that works for every one of
        them is the DUPLICATE OLD VALUE, and it works because it
        never asks what the value means.

   R4. "An operation that did not change the value cannot have
        lost an update."  RETRACTED.  'or $0, (%rbx)' writes
        back exactly the word it read, so the value is right
        whatever the interleaving, and the duplicate test still
        fires -- two threads were handed the same old value, and
        that is a fact about the INSTRUCTION rather than about
        the data.  'The value is unchanged' and 'the operation
        was atomic' are different claims and every instrument in
        this collection except this one conflates them.

   R5. "The three-operand VEX form removed an instruction from
        the loop and so it must be faster."  RETRACTED, and
        this one is inherited rather than new.  The assembly
        course measured it at 1.00x and retracted the claim.  This
        file ran the OTHER body -- the one where the destination
        IS also a source, so the legacy form needs no copy at
        all -- at 128 bits, and the copy is still free.  What
        the three operands bought is a SOURCE-LEVEL PROPERTY,
        and a source-level property is what an encoding is for.

   R6. "The AVX-SSE transition penalty is what vzeroupper
        exists for, and it will be visible here."  RETRACTED as
        a claim about THIS machine.  The bit pattern came out
        the other way round: on this part a LEGACY SSE
        instruction PRESERVES the upper halves and a VEX-128
        instruction ZEROES them.  And the timing table, once it
        was measuring the legacy encoding rather than VEX, showed
        the interleaved loop costing the same as the AVX loop
        alone.  The transition penalty is NOT OBSERVABLE HERE.
        The transition penalty is not the only reason the
        instruction exists and on this part the other reason is
        the bit pattern.

   R7. "The first version of the vzeroupper experiment measured
        nothing because it used intrinsics for the SSE arm."
        CONFIRMED, and it is a compiler fact rather than a
        timing fact.  `gcc -mavx2` compiles `_mm_add_ps` to
        `c5 f8 58 c1` -- VEX -- and a VEX-128 instruction has no
        AVX-SSE transition penalty AT ALL, so every arm came out
        1.00x.  The measurement was of the wrong bytes.  The arms
        in section 3C are raw 0F 58 encodings written in inline
        asm, and the build script disassembles them.

   R8. "A static array is a safe place to probe a load."  
        RETRACTED.  The alignment probe used a static array
        filled by a loop, and the compiler folded the whole
        experiment: the `vmovapd` became a `vmovsd` of a value
        it had already read at compile time, and the probe
        reported OK for an aligned load AND for a misaligned
        one.  A compiler that knows the CONTENTS cannot be asked
        to measure what is in them.  The buffer is mmap'd now.

   R9. "A round trip proves the encoder is right."  RETRACTED.
        The first encoder in this course round-tripped 28 of 28
        against its own decoder and was wrong: it wrote the
        two-byte VEX with the THREE-byte field order, putting X~
        and B~ where v~3 and v~2 belong.  A round trip is a
        tautology -- two readers built from one table will agree
        whether or not the table is.  It took objdump, which was
        not written from that table, to find it.  The artifact now
        cross-checks every row against a second reader and says
        so on every row.

  R10. "In a VEX-encoded instruction, ModRM.r/m is the
        destination."  RETRACTED for the three-operand form.
        It is the convention every GPR instruction follows and
        it is wrong here:
          Intel 'vaddps DEST, SRC1, SRC2'
            ModRM.reg = DEST   vvvv = SRC1   ModRM.rm = SRC2
        Established by assembling three DISTINCT registers and
        reading which field each landed in, which is a five-line
        experiment and the only trustworthy way to learn it.

  R11. "A probe that returns must have run."  RETRACTED.
        The exit status of a forked child is a SIGNAL, and a
        probe the COMPILER DELETED also returns cleanly.  The
        first alignment probe reported 'returned' for eight
        instructions, four of which were not in the binary.  The
        build script now disassembles the probe bodies and the
        harness asserts the mnemonics are present.

  R12. "Filling a YMM register, running one instruction and
        reading lane 4 measures the transition."  RETRACTED as
        a probe.  The fill and the read were two separate inline
        asm blocks, and between them gcc inserted a VEX-128
        instruction on ymm0 -- which ZEROES the upper half on
        this part, so the experiment reported 0x00000000 for a
        transition that never happened.  The whole sequence is
        now ONE asm block, which is the only way to observe a
        register state between two instructions.

  R13. "The three-operand form is a source-level property and
        no duration can price it."  NOT RETRACTED, and it is
        printed here because the one retraction in this course
        that survives is a NEGATIVE claim, and negative claims
        are the ones that get quietly dropped.  Three bodies,
        three widths, 1.00x, and the copy is free.

  R16. "A duplicate-old-value test can decide whether a
        read-modify-write is atomic."  RETRACTED, and it took
        four versions to find the instrument that works.  A
        PER-THREAD hash set reported N-1 duplicates for every
        arm including `lock or $0`, because a thread that is
        outrun sees its own value again -- which happens for an
        ATOMIC read-modify-write too.  A SHARED set needs
        synchronisation and had none, so it was a data race in
        the instrument while it measured data races.  The version
        that put the incrementer and the probe on the SAME word
        counted CLOBBERS: a value-preserving probe writes a stale
        value back and undoes an increment the other thread had
        already made.  What works is the PAIRED COUNTER -- thread
        0 increments, thread 1 probes the same word, and the
        observable is a lost-update COUNT, which is a quantity
        instrument 1 already measures correctly.  Section 4
        writes all four out, with the number each one produced.

  R17. "A CMPXCHG loop's accumulator pair is an input."
        RETRACTED.  EDX:EAX is an INPUT *AND AN OUTPUT* pair: on
        a failed compare the CPU writes the memory value back
        into it, and THE LOOP NEEDS THAT WRITE.  Declared as plain
        inputs -- which the first version did, because `+a` on a
        `uint64_t` local looked like enough -- the compiler
        reloads the same stale value every iteration, the compare
        never succeeds, the loop gives up and the counter reads
        1.  Four rows of the CNT table were wrong for this and
        three of them printed a number that looked like a
        result.  The 8-byte and 16-byte forms also need BOTH
        halves of the new value bound: the new value is EBX:ECX
        and RBX:RCX, and a half-bound one is whatever the
        register allocator left in the other half.

  R18. "An arm is atomic when the total is exactly 2N."
        RETRACTED, and the correction is a table of THREE exact
        totals rather than one.  An arm that moves the value UP
        ends at 2N.  One that moves it DOWN ends at 2^32 - 2N,
        because the word is 32 bits, and a table that expected
        2N reported a perfectly atomic `lock dec` as non-atomic.
        One that TOGGLES a bit ends at 0, because the two threads
        toggle an even number of times between them.  Getting any
        of the three wrong calls a working arm broken, and the
        EXPECTATION is the thing that has to be right before a
        measurement can be called a pass.

  R19. "A table with twenty perfect rows is evidence."
        RETRACTED.  It was the output of an instrument whose
        probe thread had fallen through to `default` and done
        NOTHING while the incrementer ran alone, so nothing was
        lost and every arm read ATOMIC.  An instrument that
        measured nothing reports no losses, and a table of
        perfect results is what a broken instrument looks like
        from the outside.  The CLOCK column section 4 prints
        beside every count exists so a reader can check that the
        instrument moved.

  R20. "BTS and BTR are atomic when the final word is 1 and 0."
        RETRACTED: they are NOT TESTABLE with this instrument, at
        all, locked or not.  BTS SETS bit 0 and BTR CLEARS it, and
        both are IDEMPOTENT, so a lost update is invisible -- the
        word ends at exactly 1 however many updates were lost.
        The paired instrument cannot help either, because it
        restores the same bit.  Only BTC, which TOGGLES, can be
        read this way, and it is the only member of the group
        whose row means anything.  That is a limit of the
        INSTRUMENT and not a fact about the instructions, and
        the artifact says so rather than quoting the other two
        rows as a result.

  R21. "A checksum loop over a byte array is safe to let the
        compiler vectorise."  RETRACTED.  It is the CHECKSUM
        that is supposed to catch a bad copy, and a vectorised
        32-byte load at offset 4092 of a 4096-byte buffer is a
        clean SIGSEGV.  The artifact died there, four kilobytes
        into a run that had already printed two hundred correct
        lines.  The loops are volatile and the buffers are
        oversized, and both of those are load-bearing.

  R22. "MFENCE and SFENCE differ in a single bit position."
        RETRACTED.  They are 0f ae f0 and 0f ae f8, so the ModRM
        reg field differs in TWO bit positions and not one: MFENCE
        has bit 3 clear where SFENCE has it set, while bits 2 and
        1 are clear in both.  And the more interesting statement
        is the OPPOSITE of the draft's: MFENCE and SFENCE share
        the reg value 7 and are told apart by the ESCAPE, so a
        decoder that switches on ModRM.reg alone gets them right
        only by accident.

  R23. "The objdump cross-check agreed with the encoder on
        28 of 30 rows."  RETRACTED, and this is the worst of
        the twenty-four, because it was BELIEVED.  The real
        reading of the first run was 28 DISAGREEMENTS out of 30,
        and this file reported them as evidence that the
        encoder was broken.  The encoder was fine.  The reader
        of the DISASSEMBLY was off by one row: objdump prints a
        section header before the first instruction, that line
        has no mnemonic in it, and the loader counted it as
        row 0.  Every row was therefore compared against its
        NEIGHBOUR's text, and the two rows that reported
        agreement did so only because both of them happen to
        be vaddps.  THE LESSON, and it is worth more than the
        thirty bytes: a cross-check that disagrees with almost
        everything is as SUSPICIOUS as one that agrees with
        everything.  The two failure modes a misaligned reader
        produces -- all-disagree and all-agree -- look nothing
        like each other and are equally wrong, and the only
        reason this one was caught is that the number was
        checked against what a CORRECT run looks like and not
        against whether it felt right.  This is the same fault
        as R11, one layer up: a probe that returns must have
        run, and a cross-check that fails must have been aimed.

  R24. "The arm labelled 'store to my line, then load the
        peer's' is a two-thread ping-pong."  RETRACTED, and it
        took THREE attempts to fix, which is why it is here at
        that length.
        ONE.  The body took a THREAD ID and never used it: both
        threads addressed mine[0] and peer[0], so 'my line'
        and 'the peer's' were the SAME two words and the arm
        was the one-cache-line case wearing the wrong name.
        The floor arm, which stores and does NOT load, was not
        a floor either, because its single store was already
        contended; it measured half a same-line ping-pong and
        came out the SAME SIZE as the full one.
        TWO.  Indexing by id was not enough.  The arrays were
        two words wide with aligned(64) on them, and that
        attribute aligns the ARRAY and not each ELEMENT, so
        the two threads' words were still EIGHT bytes apart and
        still shared a line.  aligned(64) is not a promise
        about the elements; it is a promise about the object.
        THREE.  The fix is a STRIDE: a line is 64 bytes and a
        uint64_t is 8, so each thread gets a row of eight.
        THAT is the one that worked, and the layout is now
        PRINTED above the table and asserted by the harness,
        because two of the three were layout bugs that produced
        numbers which looked entirely reasonable.
        THE TELL, and it is the part worth keeping: the
        store-only floor came out ABOVE the ping-pong in every
        run.  That is impossible for a floor, and this file
        PRINTED THE RATIO instead of treating it as a
        contradiction of its own vocabulary.  A row's NAME is a
        claim about what the row did, and a name is not
        evidence -- which is R3 and R23 again, and the reason
        this course spends three paragraphs on what an
        instrument is.  A number that cannot have come out is
        the friendliest thing an experiment ever does, and the
        only way to deserve it is to hold a claim that says
        the number should have been SMALLER.

  R14. "A not-equal, not-0xFFFFFFFF, not-0x40000000 immediate
        is a different test from 0."  NOT A RETRACTION BUT THE
        FINDING THAT REPLACED ONE.  The involution arms were
        rewritten to use immediates that CHANGE the value, which
        is what makes the counter instrument applicable to them
        at all.  XOR with 0 is the identity and can never lose an
        update; XOR with 0x40000000 toggles a bit and can.


  AND THE ONES THAT ARE NOT ABOUT THE ARCHITECTURE AT ALL
  are R8, R11, R16, R17, R19, R21, R23 and R24, and they
  are the eight a reader should take away from this
  course: every one of them is a measurement that was
  WRONG about the machine and RIGHT about its own
  instrument.  Six of the twenty-four are mistakes about
  what a NUMBER is (R2, R4, R18, R19, R20 and R22),
  SEVEN are about what an INSTRUMENT is (R3, R8, R11,
  R16, R21, R23 and R24), three are about what an
  ENCODING is (R9, R10 and R15), and the rest are about
  what a BINARY contains or what a SPECIFICATION says.
  Not one of the twenty-four is a mistake about how a
  computer works, and that is the argument the course
  ends on.


7B. THE LIMITS.  Printed rather than footnoted, because a limit
  that is a footnote is a limit that gets forgotten.

  NO EVENT COUNTER OF ANY KIND.  perf_event_paranoid is 4,
  RDPMC is a fault, perf_event_open is EACCES, and
  /dev/cpu/0/msr is root-only.  Nothing in this file counts
  instructions, cycles, uops, cache lines or coherence
  transactions.  Every duration is a DURATION, and a duration
  BOUNDS A COUNT WITHOUT MEASURING IT.  Every mechanism named
  in this file is therefore marked INFERRED unless it is a
  fault, a bit pattern or a byte.  Settled by perf_event_open
  with a paranoid setting below 4 and a PMU passthrough.

  NO AVX-512.  CPUID.7.0:EBX F, DQ, CD, BW and VL are five
  zeros and the XCR0 opmask, zmm_hi256 and hi16_zmm bits are
  three more.  So EVERYTHING about a ZMM register, about a
  k0-k7 mask and about the four-level dispatch is QUOTED and
  marked so.  What IS measured is that the bytes decode, which
  needs no silicon.  Settled by a Skylake-SP or a Zen 4.

  ONLY ONE MISALIGNMENT WAS MEASURED, at 8 bytes.  A load that
  is 8-byte aligned and not 16-byte aligned is the case that
  matters and it is the case that ran; nothing here says what
  happens at 4, 20 or 28, and the alignment requirement is a
  requirement on the SIZE not the offset.

  WHETHER ANY LOCKED INSTRUCTION ASSERTED LOCK# ON THE BUS.
  On every processor since the P6 a locked instruction whose
  line is already in the local cache does not drive the pin at
  all; only the cache is locked.  What is measured is the
  OBSERVABLE BEHAVIOUR, which is the thing a program relies
  on.  Settled by a logic analyser on the pin.

  THE COST OF A LOCKED INSTRUCTION, AND THE COST OF A LOCKED
  16-BYTE ONE.  Section 4 establishes that CMPXCHG16b is
  ATOMIC on 16 bytes and it does not establish what that costs.
  The smp course measured an uncontended lock against a plain
  store and that is the number to quote, not this one.

  THE STORE-BUFFER DEPTH, AND WHETHER SFENCE ACTUALLY CHANGES
  THE PING-PONG'S SHAPE.  Section 5A measures the COST of the
  fence and does not observe the buffer.  The mechanism -- that
  a store does not invalidate the peer's copy until it
  retires -- is INFERRED from a duration that did not behave
  the way the naive story predicts.  Settled by a PMU.

  WHY THE DOWNWARD STRING DIRECTION IS SLOWER.  Measured, four
  sizes, a shape.  NOT EXPLAINED: there is no counter that can
  count the micro-ops a REP MOVSB expands to and the vendor
  manual gives no number either.

  A SECOND RUN'S RATIOS.  The exact figures move between runs;
  the SHAPES do not, and every figure in the harness is
  asserted as an ordering or a shape for that reason.

  AArch64, RISC-V, and every other architecture.  LDXR/STXR and
  the DMB that orders them are a different instruction set with
  a different failure mode -- an LL/SC pair can ALWAYS fail and
  there is no way to make it not -- and the comparison belongs
  to the smp course, which owns the principle.

7C. WHAT THIS ARTIFACT CAN SUPPORT.

  Each line below is a claim this file MEASURED, and the
  list was checked against the twenty-four retractions: the
  first version of this block asserted that the VEX form
  removed the alignment requirement, which R15 retracts and
  section 2 measures the other way, and a verdict that
  contradicts a retraction in the same file is worse than
  no verdict at all.

  * Which of eighteen instructions behave atomically on this
    part, and which of them imply LOCK, MEASURED, and not
    quoted.
  * That the aligned vector loads fault at +8 bytes and the
    unaligned ones do not -- and that the VEX form does NOT
    remove the requirement, which is the opposite of what
    the first version of this block said.
  * What the upper 128 bits of a YMM register hold after each
    class of instruction, as a BIT PATTERN, which needs no timer.
  * The exact bytes of thirty instructions, agreed on by two
    readers that were written independently of each other.
  * That the three fences are ONE opcode, 0F AE, with a
    three-bit selector in which MFENCE and SFENCE COLLIDE at
    reg=7 and are told apart by the escape.  NOT 'they differ
    in one field', which is the error R22 retracts.
  * That the direction flag is at bit 10, and that a backward
    REP MOVSB is still slower than a forward one here.

7D. WHAT IT CANNOT SUPPORT.

  * Any claim about AVX-512 EXECUTION, on any machine, because
    there is no such machine in this file.
  * Any cycle count, any event count, and any statement of the
    form 'this instruction retires in N cycles'.
  * Any claim that a locked instruction is SLOWER than an
    unlocked one, which is a claim about a noise floor and not
    about an instruction.
  * Any claim about a cache hierarchy, a prefetcher or a branch
    predictor.  The memory course owns those.


  rows measured: 207
  checksums folded in: 0xd93c0879198dc3fd
